Scene.description:
What a view draws and how it answers input: a space, marks in drawing order, a layout, a theme,
controls, how long changes ease, and the title written over it. Plain data: as JSON, each field by
its name in camelCase, each mark `{ vertices | edges | faces | lines | areas: options }`.

```
# use latkit::{Colormap, Edges, Earth, Scene, Vertices, field};
let voltage = field("vm").colormap(Colormap::BALANCE).domain(0.95..=1.05);
let scene = Scene::new(Earth::globe())
    .draw(Edges::of("grid", "Branch").ends("from", "to"))
    .draw(Vertices::of("grid", "Bus").position("lon", "lat").color(voltage).legend("Bus voltage · pu"))
    .title("Voltage");
```

keyboard.description:
Tab steps through the rows nearest the view's middle and arrows to the nearest row their way;
Enter opens the row stepped to and Space selects it. Otherwise arrows pan, `+` and `-` zoom,
Home fits, and Escape clears the selection.

Shot.description:
A picture of views, each drawn into its panel of `size` pixels at `scale` pixels per CSS pixel:
plain data, like a scene.

```no_run
# async fn example(view: &mut latkit::View) -> latkit::Result<()> {
use latkit::Shot;
let image = Shot::new([3840, 2160]).scale(2.0).image(&mut [view]).await?;
std::fs::write("view.png", image.encode(latkit::ImageFormat::Png)?).expect("written");
# Ok(()) }
```

Panels.description:
Where each of several views draws in a target: x, y, width, and height as fractions of it, a
panel per view, in order; a later panel draws over an earlier one. None given is one view filling
the target.

```
use latkit::Panels;
let map_over_plot = Panels::from([[0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.8], [0.0, 0.8, 1.0, 0.2]]);
assert_eq!(map_over_plot.len(), 2);
assert_eq!(Panels::default().len(), 1);
```
